1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It lifts the spirits and intoxicates the senses.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Gods were created for it. Now robots bottle it.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
And sometimes its cost can make a prince a pauper.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Not bad for something that we shake, stomp, and hide underground.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Wine on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,080
It's known simply as Crush, the annual fall harvest of grapes that marks the beginning

7
00:00:39,081 --> 00:00:41,660
of California's winemaking season.

8
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:44,600
We usually start harvesting grapes in September.

9
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:49,820
It's a time of year where all of our hard work from an entire year kind of comes to

10
00:00:49,821 --> 00:00:50,820
fruition.

11
00:00:50,821 --> 00:00:54,440
It's kind of the Super Bowl for us.

12
00:00:54,441 --> 00:01:00,540
year in winemaking regions around the world, millions of tons of grapes are gathered, either

13
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:09,740
by hand or by powerful mechanical harvesters that shake the vines into submission.

14
00:01:09,741 --> 00:01:22,340
In California alone, more than three million tons of wine grapes are harvested annually.

15
00:01:22,341 --> 00:01:27,020
The decision to machine or hand pick is just one of many that every winemaker must make.

16
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:36,220
At Buena Vista Carneros, for example, the harvest is done only by hand and occurs at night to preserve freshness.

17
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:42,580
The warmer the fruit comes into the winery, usually the less fruit intensity you'll have in the final wine.

18
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:47,980
So we're really, really big fans of getting out there when it's cold, get the fruit in here and take care of it from there.

19
00:01:47,981 --> 00:01:53,420
At the winery, the grape clusters are quickly sorted.

20
00:01:53,421 --> 00:01:57,540
Then they fall into a de-stemmer, where a series of beater bars gently knock the grapes

21
00:01:57,541 --> 00:02:02,920
from their stems.

22
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:09,060
Once separated and clean, the grapes are now ready to become wine.

23
00:02:09,061 --> 00:02:13,620
Grape vines are among nature's great survivors, and often thrive in places where little else

24
00:02:13,621 --> 00:02:16,620
will grow.

25
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:21,220
Initially divided into red and white, there are literally thousands of grape varieties

26
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:23,580
in the world, although only six.

27
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:32,100
Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Merlot, and Cabernet Sauvignon account

28
00:02:32,101 --> 00:02:34,500
for 80% of all American wine sales.

29
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:43,600
Each grape imparts different flavors into the wine, depending on how juicy the grape

30
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:45,300
is, how thick the skin is.

31
00:02:45,301 --> 00:02:48,820
Some of the grapes are very aromatic, some are not.

32
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,880
Some of them have better color, more tannin, the astringent puckery flavor that's common to wine.

33
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:01,700
But no matter their color or variety, all wine grapes will share a common destiny.

34
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,420
Fermentation, one of nature's remarkable processes.

35
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:15,100
As the grapes grow on the vine, they develop sugars within as a byproduct of photosynthesis.

36
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:20,580
only the grapes elastic skin protects these sugars from a naturally occurring yeast

37
00:03:20,581 --> 00:03:22,440
that forms on the grapes exterior

38
00:03:22,441 --> 00:03:30,580
when the grape skin is broken or crushed the yeasts assault the sugars consuming them in a fury

39
00:03:30,581 --> 00:03:35,960
that results in alcohol if you took these grapes and would put them in a bucket full in the back

40
00:03:35,961 --> 00:03:40,960
of your car and you stomped them a little bit about a week later in your garage you'd have

41
00:03:40,961 --> 00:03:44,360
some wine being made that's pretty it's pretty amazing

42
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:55,480
Today, presses of all sizes are used to extract the grape juice and begin fermentation.

43
00:03:55,481 --> 00:04:00,640
To what degree that juice remains in contact with the grape skins, and even seeds, is crucial

44
00:04:00,641 --> 00:04:04,460
to the character of any wine.

45
00:04:04,461 --> 00:04:07,900
With a white grape, we'll separate the juice and the skins immediately, and that juice

46
00:04:07,901 --> 00:04:09,740
will ferment by itself.

47
00:04:09,741 --> 00:04:12,760
With reds, we actually ferment the skins and the juice all together.

48
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,660
Because everything that makes a red wine what it is, is in the skins.

49
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:22,640
The juice itself is clear, it's colorless, it doesn't have the true flavor of the varietal.

50
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,800
So the character is really in the skin.

51
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:36,560
French scientist Louis Pasteur was the first to clearly define the biological process of fermentation.

52
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:48,520
Although the mystery of wine fermentation had taken generations to solve, its intoxicating

53
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:50,880
effects have been known throughout time.

54
00:04:50,881 --> 00:04:59,780
Well, they found sunken jars in present-day Iran that they dated to about 4,000 to 5,000

55
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:03,820
years BC.

56
00:05:03,821 --> 00:05:08,600
Nearly every civilization since has made wine a centerpiece in its religious and cultural

57
00:05:08,601 --> 00:05:11,180
life.

58
00:05:11,181 --> 00:05:15,940
The Greeks and Egyptians spread wine making throughout the known world, and the Romans

59
00:05:15,941 --> 00:05:18,920
became its first great merchants.

60
00:05:18,921 --> 00:05:24,060
But throughout the ages, no civilization has been more passionately associated with wine

61
00:05:24,061 --> 00:05:26,640
than France.

62
00:05:26,641 --> 00:05:31,740
Beginning in the 11th century AD, French monks were the first to make detailed studies of

63
00:05:31,741 --> 00:05:37,540
the complex relationship between wine and the soil and the climate in which it grows.

64
00:05:37,541 --> 00:05:43,740
Over time, the monks walled off parcels of land that consistently produced wines of greater

65
00:05:43,741 --> 00:05:46,260
subtlety and flavor.

66
00:05:46,261 --> 00:05:49,980
Parcels that today still make the most expensive wines in the world.

67
00:05:49,981 --> 00:05:54,500
Remarkably, many of those vineyards were located in the thin mineral soils along slopes and

68
00:05:54,501 --> 00:05:57,680
ridges, where growing conditions were harder.

69
00:05:57,681 --> 00:05:59,980
This reflected a concept we now call stress.

70
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:05,580
And it's a concept that winemakers and grape growers come back to because a vine that struggles

71
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:09,900
really does produce wine with more intensity and more color and more structure.

72
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:14,580
And the stress from the rocky soil here, from the lack of water,

73
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:17,220
from the wind that we see on a ridgetop like this,

74
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:19,960
really adds to the intensity of a wine.

75
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:26,720
Buena Vista Carneros is the oldest premier winery in California.

76
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,840
But in the 1990s, a wave of the vine-killing insect Phylloxera

77
00:06:31,841 --> 00:06:34,320
swept through the Napa and Sonoma Valleys,

78
00:06:34,321 --> 00:06:37,860
forcing Buena Vista to replant 30% of their vineyards.

79
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:44,120
Phylloxera became a problem in California in the early 90s,

80
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:48,740
so they had to end up replanting a lot of the vineyards all at the same time,

81
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,620
which was very costly.

82
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:57,060
But it also gave them the opportunity to capitalize on the improvements in viticulture

83
00:06:57,061 --> 00:07:00,580
in the last 20 years of the 20th century.

84
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:08,080
One of the most important improvements is precision farming.

85
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:16,220
High-tech tools like digital aerial photography give those at Buena Vista a view of their vineyards otherwise invisible to the naked eye.

86
00:07:16,221 --> 00:07:20,500
Our background is actually in remote sensing of other planets.

87
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:25,480
So studying the moon and Mars, NASA technology.

88
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:30,520
And so we wanted to apply that same technology to the Earth.

89
00:07:30,521 --> 00:07:42,371
Using images taken at 10,000 feet with a four-band digital camera, companies like Vineview Imaging create a series of maps based on thermal, near-infrared, and other

90
00:07:42,372 --> 00:07:43,520
color spectrums.

91
00:07:43,521 --> 00:07:47,520
These maps reveal much about the condition of the vineyards.

92
00:07:47,521 --> 00:07:55,520
For example, because of the intense sun, vines must cool themselves or their basic chemical functions begin to fail.

93
00:07:55,521 --> 00:07:57,960
begin to fail.

94
00:07:57,961 --> 00:08:01,460
Healthy vines will evaporate water through their leaves and roots

95
00:08:01,461 --> 00:08:05,140
and appear in this stress map in light green and yellow.

96
00:08:05,141 --> 00:08:09,380
Vines that are struggling are in the red and brown regions,

97
00:08:09,381 --> 00:08:12,140
while the dark green areas are under stressed

98
00:08:12,141 --> 00:08:15,600
and not working hard enough.

99
00:08:15,601 --> 00:08:17,440
Bringing all the vines into better balance

100
00:08:17,441 --> 00:08:20,320
may mean a simple adjustment in irrigation.

101
00:08:20,321 --> 00:08:22,380
However, if the pattern persists,

102
00:08:22,381 --> 00:08:25,080
it may also signal a need to replant the block

103
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:28,540
with a different type or variety of grape.

104
00:08:28,541 --> 00:08:30,460
And what was once a single vineyard

105
00:08:30,461 --> 00:08:32,420
must then be farmed as many.

106
00:08:32,421 --> 00:08:34,960
Well, right here today across the 800 acres that are planted,

107
00:08:34,961 --> 00:08:37,660
we have 167 different blocks.

108
00:08:37,661 --> 00:08:39,200
And each of those blocks, we really

109
00:08:39,201 --> 00:08:40,600
farm as an individual vineyard.

110
00:08:40,601 --> 00:08:43,060
So we don't think of this as an 800 acre vineyard.

111
00:08:43,061 --> 00:08:45,260
It's really 167 little vineyards.

112
00:08:45,261 --> 00:08:47,240
And you have to approach it that way,

113
00:08:47,241 --> 00:08:49,180
because the needs of this block are very different

114
00:08:49,181 --> 00:08:51,800
from the block next door, where you have different soil types,

115
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:54,580
you have different water holding capacities.

116
00:08:54,581 --> 00:08:56,980
And that's really the beauty of what we've done here.

117
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:05,060
Construction of each new vineyard block requires other high-tech tools to precisely define their physical layout.

118
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:11,060
Basically what I do is I come out to a new development vineyard or even an established vineyard,

119
00:09:11,061 --> 00:09:19,120
and I use the GPS to shoot off the satellites in space to map out the area that they're going to plant the vineyard.

120
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:24,200
GPS units lock in where the vine rows are spaced and planted.

121
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:28,820
and also position irrigation pipelines, manholes, and other practical elements.

122
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:36,180
Once this work is completed, all that is needed are the vines themselves.

123
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:42,980
This is a typical grafted grapevine that a vineyard here would use to plant for their vineyard.

124
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:47,780
And it's composed of really two different plants. One is the rootstock,

125
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:53,620
which is the bottom part here, and then we call the signwood, budwood, or we call fruitingwood on the top.

126
00:09:54,580 --> 00:10:01,580
When young, these two independent vines are joined together in a process known as bench grafting.

127
00:10:01,581 --> 00:10:09,580
The new graft is dipped in wax for protection, then raised for one year in the nursery,

128
00:10:09,581 --> 00:10:12,580
until it's replanted in the vineyard.

129
00:10:12,581 --> 00:10:17,580
This point here, here's where the graft was made at Mr. Herrick's nursery.

130
00:10:17,581 --> 00:10:20,580
And from this point down, obviously, is the rootstock.

131
00:10:20,581 --> 00:10:24,480
And then we crafted a cyan or budwood on top of this.

132
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:26,500
In this case, it was Pinot Noir.

133
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:31,040
There are 16 distinct rootstocks grown at Herrick's,

134
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:36,260
each offering the winemaker a potential match for the various soil conditions at his winery.

135
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:43,460
The fruiting wood is chosen both for the type of wine the winemaker wants to produce,

136
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:47,740
as well as for the climate and weather of the region where the winery is found.

137
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:51,340
Here in Carneros, it's a very cool region.

138
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:54,580
You can see today it's a fairly gray morning.

139
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:55,640
We get a lot of fog.

140
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:58,980
Very, very cool weather typically compared to some other growing regions.

141
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:03,780
So that's driven us to really focus on what we consider cool climate varietals.

142
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:08,840
Chardonnay, Pinot Noir are the classic cool climate varietals around the world.

143
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:13,240
But even then, within varieties like Pinot Noir or Chardonnay,

144
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:16,740
there exist distinct strains known as clones.

145
00:11:16,741 --> 00:11:24,420
In the vineyards at Buena Vista, there are currently 25 different Pinot Noir clones planted.

146
00:11:24,421 --> 00:11:28,740
But each of these clones will have a different characteristic, different flavors, maybe a

147
00:11:28,741 --> 00:11:30,378
different ripening pattern.

148
00:11:30,379 --> 00:11:34,260
And the whole goal for us is to match up that exact clone on the

149
00:11:34,261 --> 00:11:38,267
perfect spot. And that's how you make the best wines.

150
00:11:38,268 --> 00:11:41,140
Grape vines require four years to bear

151
00:11:41,141 --> 00:11:44,968
usable fruit. The first grape buds appear in March.

152
00:11:44,969 --> 00:11:47,520
For the next seven months, growers

153
00:11:47,521 --> 00:11:51,740
keep a diligent eye over every inch of the vineyard, constantly measuring growth and

154
00:11:51,741 --> 00:11:52,740
stress patterns.

155
00:11:52,741 --> 00:11:58,560
Six inches of depth, we have a very, very dry 18% moisture.

156
00:11:58,561 --> 00:12:03,500
By the fall, each block of grapes has developed unique characteristics of flavor, acids,

157
00:12:03,501 --> 00:12:10,860
tannins, and color.

158
00:12:10,861 --> 00:12:15,280
These blocks are then harvested and fermented separately in order to form the building blocks

159
00:12:15,281 --> 00:12:16,580
of the wine to come.

160
00:12:16,581 --> 00:12:20,920
Well, by keeping each vineyard block or little section of the vineyard separate through the

161
00:12:20,921 --> 00:12:25,380
winemaking process, it allows us to put different blends together at the end of the year.

162
00:12:25,381 --> 00:12:29,180
You might have one section of the vineyard that gives you really great cherry and berry

163
00:12:29,181 --> 00:12:33,400
flavors, and you might have another section that gives you really good kind of earthy

164
00:12:33,401 --> 00:12:34,400
complex flavors.

165
00:12:34,401 --> 00:12:38,540
Well, we can take those wines, blend them with all their brothers and sisters, and come up

166
00:12:38,541 --> 00:12:40,340
with a wine that's more complex, more interesting,

167
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:44,300
and in the end, tastes better in the glass.

168
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,460
What took the French monks centuries to discern

169
00:12:48,461 --> 00:12:53,100
can now be manipulated and fine-tuned in a matter of years.

170
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:57,160
In less than a decade after the Phylloxera wave,

171
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:00,880
Buena Vista Carneros is again making award-winning wines.

