00:17:51,561 --> 00:17:58,680
A series of 30 to 50-ton presses turn the grapes into juice.

227
00:17:58,681 --> 00:18:05,580
A typical press is a large cylindrical container with a rubber membrane affixed to the interior

228
00:18:05,581 --> 00:18:08,200
walls.

229
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:13,320
membrane is steadily inflated with air pressure to extract the juice which flows into circular

230
00:18:13,321 --> 00:18:18,200
perforated drain channels.

231
00:18:18,201 --> 00:18:22,460
Full pressure is maintained for only a few minutes before the membrane will deflate and

232
00:18:22,461 --> 00:18:27,960
the residue will be removed.

233
00:18:27,961 --> 00:18:33,720
High powered centrifuges then eliminate nearly all remaining solid waste, enhancing the quality

234
00:18:33,721 --> 00:18:38,400
and speed of the wine's development.

235
00:18:38,401 --> 00:18:45,240
Fermentation occurs over 12 days within this sea of 45 to 54 foot tall storage tanks, capable

236
00:18:45,241 --> 00:18:51,400
of holding more than 80 million gallons of wine.

237
00:18:51,401 --> 00:18:56,060
Once ready, the wine is shipped to another Bronco facility, where it is quality checked,

238
00:18:56,061 --> 00:18:58,420
bottled, and then corped.

239
00:18:58,421 --> 00:18:59,660
We call them jaws.

240
00:18:59,661 --> 00:19:04,420
It's like a diaphragm, almost in a camera, because what it will do is it'll squeeze or

241
00:19:04,421 --> 00:19:09,660
constrict around the cork, and then it squeezes it smaller than the opening of the bottle,

242
00:19:09,661 --> 00:19:10,660
right?

243
00:19:10,661 --> 00:19:17,420
And then a plunger, just a metal rod, comes down and shoves the cork into the bottle.

244
00:19:17,421 --> 00:19:21,860
When Bronco owner Fred Franzia's grandfather opened his first winery in the San Joaquin

245
00:19:21,861 --> 00:19:26,860
Valley, the hope was to sell a good bottle of wine at a fair price.

246
00:19:26,861 --> 00:19:32,860
Today, Bronco delivers on that dream at a rate of 240 bottles per minute.

247
00:19:32,861 --> 00:19:39,860
I'm a firm believer the consumer is the smartest person out there and he knows what he wants.

248
00:19:39,861 --> 00:19:46,860
And we've got to find out, as suppliers of the consumer, the products they want and what they want to pay for it.

249
00:19:46,861 --> 00:19:50,860
And if you give them good quality and good value, they find it.

250
00:19:50,861 --> 00:20:02,200
In 1972, lawyer Jim Barrett purchased Chateau Montalena and began to revitalize the winery.

251
00:20:02,201 --> 00:20:06,240
In only a few years, his efforts would make history.

252
00:20:06,241 --> 00:20:14,620
In 1976, a British wine merchant in Paris organized a blind tasting test that pitted

253
00:20:14,621 --> 00:20:20,280
world-class historic French wines against the new wave of American upstarts, including

254
00:20:20,281 --> 00:20:22,558
a 1973 Montelena Chardonnay.

255
00:20:22,559 --> 00:20:27,520
Despite a panel of all French judges, the Americans won every

256
00:20:27,521 --> 00:20:34,163
category. They beat the French wines, and this was quite a startling thing.

257
00:20:34,164 --> 00:20:34,960
Now known

258
00:20:34,961 --> 00:20:40,100
as the Judgment of Paris, this single event transformed world perception of California

259
00:20:40,101 --> 00:20:41,531
wines overnight.

260
00:20:41,532 --> 00:20:48,240
That one event, that tasting in the summer of 1976, suddenly the barricades

261
00:20:48,241 --> 00:20:53,266
to marketing of handcrafted California wine dropped in one day.

262
00:20:53,267 --> 00:20:55,580
Today, Montalena's commitment

263
00:20:55,581 --> 00:21:02,125
to hand crafting wines extends from their exceptional vineyards to the winery.

264
00:21:02,126 --> 00:21:02,880
No detail

265
00:21:02,881 --> 00:21:05,120
in the process is overlooked.

266
00:21:05,121 --> 00:21:13,380
When fermentation is complete, all of Matalena's wines are stored in oak barrels.

267
00:21:13,381 --> 00:21:24,560
Whites for eight to nine months, reds for upwards of two years.

268
00:21:24,561 --> 00:21:29,680
Many of these barrels are handcrafted at Sagan Moreau, one of the world's historic cooperages.

269
00:21:29,681 --> 00:21:37,660
No glue or nails are used to construct them, and the type of oak from which they're made

270
00:21:37,661 --> 00:21:41,140
will produce distinct flavors in the wine.

271
00:21:41,141 --> 00:21:44,880
French oak has quite a bit of tannin that it brings to the wine.

272
00:21:44,881 --> 00:21:51,400
It brings sweetness, complexity, and structure, which are all things that are good for big

273
00:21:51,401 --> 00:21:53,360
red wines or white wines.

274
00:21:53,361 --> 00:21:54,860
It gives them longevity.

275
00:21:54,861 --> 00:21:57,660
It gives them length in the bottle.

276
00:21:57,661 --> 00:22:00,800
The wine oak is known for its aromatic characters.

277
00:22:00,801 --> 00:22:03,160
A lot of vanilla and spice.

278
00:22:03,161 --> 00:22:06,680
And it sort of is also known, it fills in the middle of a wine

279
00:22:06,681 --> 00:22:07,381
very nicely.

280
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:13,740
As the wines age in the oak, Barrett and his team

281
00:22:13,741 --> 00:22:14,920
taste them constantly.

282
00:22:18,280 --> 00:22:19,560
Cruising along nicely.

283
00:22:19,561 --> 00:22:23,440
Take a look and be sober and see what we think.

284
00:22:23,441 --> 00:22:26,060
Once the wines have reached the desired richness,

285
00:22:26,061 --> 00:22:30,060
Blending begins, a process that takes weeks.

286
00:22:30,061 --> 00:22:32,940
Even if you like the first blend, say, man, this is fantastic,

287
00:22:32,941 --> 00:22:35,580
we still have to go back and make sure

288
00:22:35,581 --> 00:22:39,420
that we couldn't do something better.

289
00:22:39,421 --> 00:22:42,800
Altogether, the creation of a Chateau Montalena wine

290
00:22:42,801 --> 00:22:46,060
takes a minimum of one year for whites and two for reds.

291
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:52,400
That commitment to quality is a hallmark for many wines,

292
00:22:52,401 --> 00:22:55,100
including the king of them all, champagne.

293
00:22:56,060 --> 00:23:08,880
Skim just one inch of wine from the top of Bronco's biggest tank, and it's enough to make 540 cases, equal to 6,480 bottles of wine.

294
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:12,200
Wine will return on Modern Marvels.

295
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:19,640
We now return to wine on Modern Marvels.

296
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:25,920
Champagne, the king of wines.

297
00:23:26,060 --> 00:23:33,940
Born in silent and humid cellars, its sparkling character and intoxicating bubbles are the very essence of celebration.

298
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:41,740
The carbon dioxide that's formed during the fermentation process evaporates, is allowed to escape,

299
00:23:41,860 --> 00:23:46,980
but in champagne it's trapped in the product and it's only released when the bottle is opened.

300
00:23:49,240 --> 00:23:55,160
To understand this remarkable wine, champagne must be thought of as a place as well as a drink.

301
00:23:56,060 --> 00:23:59,300
a region of France with its own unique geography and history.

302
00:24:00,940 --> 00:24:04,600
Throughout the centuries, every French king maintained vineyards here,

303
00:24:04,940 --> 00:24:08,180
and the wines became a staple of their coronation ceremonies.

304
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:15,540
But Champagne is located at a more northern latitude than Quebec, Canada,

305
00:24:16,100 --> 00:24:18,820
making it one of the coldest wine centers in the world.

306
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:22,860
And because of this northern locale,

307
00:24:22,861 --> 00:24:26,560
A unique pause in the wine's development occurred during winter,

308
00:24:27,140 --> 00:24:31,520
when the cold stopped fermentation until the spring thaw.

309
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:37,700
In the beginning of spring, the temperature outside were going up.

310
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:40,880
Then the fermentation process resumed.

311
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:46,200
And one of the results, two results of the fermentation process are

312
00:24:46,201 --> 00:24:50,840
bubbles on one side, and on the other side, carbonic gas, pressure.

313
00:24:50,841 --> 00:24:56,140
Once opened, pressure within the barrels caused the wine to froth,

314
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:01,120
a phenomenon that French winemakers, including the fabled monk Dom Perignon,

315
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:03,540
spent decades trying to reverse.

316
00:25:04,100 --> 00:25:07,760
Dom Perignon was trying to remove the bubbles from champagne.

317
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:10,060
The English discovered that they liked it,

318
00:25:10,380 --> 00:25:15,340
and they've always been the best customers for champagne and sparkling wine.

319
00:25:15,341 --> 00:25:18,960
and probably without their interest,

320
00:25:19,260 --> 00:25:21,620
there wouldn't even be champagne as we know it.

321
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:24,600
It would be still wine, no bubbles.

322
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:31,460
In 1811, the French Champagne House of Perrier-Jouette was founded,

323
00:25:31,980 --> 00:25:34,940
owned appropriately of a marriage between the daughter of a vineyard owner

324
00:25:34,941 --> 00:25:36,280
and a corkmaker.

325
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:41,760
They immediately began to export their champagnes to England.

326
00:25:41,761 --> 00:25:48,760
By then, champagne was cellared in bottles rather than barrels, which captured the CO2

327
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:53,260
gases, in essence carbonating the wine.

328
00:25:53,261 --> 00:25:58,380
In less than a generation, Queen Victoria herself had embraced Perrier Jouette and it

329
00:25:58,381 --> 00:26:01,520
has remained a favorite in British circles ever since.

330
00:26:01,521 --> 00:26:11,480
To me, the best reward is the letter we received from Oscar Wilde in 1888.

331
00:26:11,481 --> 00:26:18,620
he has been thrown in jail and he were asking in jail jail is nothing but what

332
00:26:18,621 --> 00:26:26,120
is so difficult to survive is the lack of champagne page way

333
00:26:26,121 --> 00:26:33,320
champagne is made from three varieties Chardonnay Pinot Noir and Pinot Meunier

334
00:26:33,321 --> 00:26:44,280
We have 65 hectares, classified 99.2% on the quality scale of champagne.

335
00:26:44,780 --> 00:26:49,760
So basically, we have one of the most unique terroir of champagne.

336
00:26:50,920 --> 00:26:54,220
Through two pressings, fermentation and blending,

337
00:26:54,760 --> 00:26:58,340
these remarkable grapes are handled just as they would be for still wines.

338
00:26:58,341 --> 00:27:06,200
juice from the first pressing known as the Cuvée is considered the finest in champagne

339
00:27:06,201 --> 00:27:12,080
consistency is prized above all else and the new Cuvée is blended with wines from previous years

340
00:27:12,081 --> 00:27:17,180
to ensure every bottle of Perrier Jouette reflects what is known as the house style

341
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:27,020
only after the blend is finished to the bubbles come a second fermentation is induced by the

342
00:27:27,021 --> 00:27:33,000
addition of new yeast and 24 grams of sugar to each bottle. The yeast again

343
00:27:33,001 --> 00:27:37,140
consumes the sugar but unlike in the frothing oak barrels of old, the

344
00:27:37,141 --> 00:27:43,400
resulting co2 remains trapped inside the wine. For three years the dying yeasts

345
00:27:43,401 --> 00:27:49,060
will continue to impart flavors to the wine. During the final year the bottles

346
00:27:49,061 --> 00:27:56,080
will be turned and elevated eight times a day in a process known as riddling.

347
00:27:57,020 --> 00:28:04,020
Riddling encourages the dead yeasts to settle into the neck of the bottle.

348
00:28:04,021 --> 00:28:09,020
Although not harmful if consumed, the yeast simply isn't very pretty, and so it's removed

349
00:28:09,021 --> 00:28:12,260
in a process known as disgorgement.

350
00:28:12,261 --> 00:28:16,980
In traditional disgorgement, a single blow of the winemaker's tool causes the cap on

351
00:28:16,981 --> 00:28:25,980
the bottle to explode, catapulting the yeast out.

352
00:28:25,981 --> 00:28:30,940
To maintain the distinct qualities of this remarkable wine, strict laws assert that only

353
00:28:30,941 --> 00:28:35,760
those wines grown and made in the Champagne region can bear the name Champagne on their

354
00:28:35,761 --> 00:28:38,540
label.

355
00:28:38,541 --> 00:28:42,980
Annual production is also limited, ensuring quality while also guaranteeing that labels

356
00:28:42,981 --> 00:28:47,780
such as Perrier Jouette will be in constant demand.

357
00:28:47,781 --> 00:28:54,100
But that hasn't stopped French winemakers from reaching out to other parts of the world,

358
00:28:54,101 --> 00:29:00,180
The technologies now bring new meaning to old methods.

359
00:29:00,181 --> 00:29:03,540
Consider the advanced automation at Mum Napa,

360
00:29:03,541 --> 00:29:06,580
producers of one of America's most popular sparkling wines.

361
00:29:09,320 --> 00:29:11,420
Here, bottles of sparkling wine are filled

362
00:29:11,421 --> 00:29:15,820
at a rate of 120 bottles per minute.

363
00:29:15,821 --> 00:29:18,160
Sophisticated computer programs oversee

364
00:29:18,161 --> 00:29:20,100
state-of-the-art riddling machines

365
00:29:20,101 --> 00:29:22,540
that move large containers of the sparkling wine

366
00:29:22,541 --> 00:29:24,300
a fraction of a turn at a time.

367
00:29:26,280 --> 00:29:28,040
Only six to eight days later,

368
00:29:28,041 --> 00:29:30,000
the neck of the bottle is frozen,

369
00:29:30,001 --> 00:29:31,740
capturing the yeast in place.

370
00:29:33,180 --> 00:29:35,060
When the bottle is mechanically opened,

371
00:29:35,061 --> 00:29:38,180
the frozen yeast pops out in the form of an ice plug.

372
00:29:39,520 --> 00:29:43,160
One of many remarkable solutions to age old problems.

373
00:29:43,161 --> 00:29:44,540
When it comes to wine,

374
00:29:44,541 --> 00:29:48,320
we use the latest progress of phenology, for sure.

375
00:29:48,321 --> 00:29:52,300
But we use it only to compliment

376
00:29:52,301 --> 00:29:54,400
tradition.

377
00:29:54,401 --> 00:29:57,780
But France isn't the only land steeped in tradition,

378
00:29:57,781 --> 00:30:00,000
nor where the past and future combine

379
00:30:00,001 --> 00:30:04,220
to make a world-class wine.

380
00:30:04,221 --> 00:30:06,540
Recent scientific studies have suggested

381
00:30:06,541 --> 00:30:11,240
that every bottle of champagne generates at least 49 million

382
00:30:11,241 --> 00:30:14,420
bubbles by the time it's finished.

383
00:30:14,421 --> 00:30:17,020
Wine will return on Modern Marvelous.

384
00:30:17,021 --> 00:30:24,020
We now return to Wine on Modern Marbles.

385
00:30:24,021 --> 00:30:29,880
They're called Stairways to Heaven, centuries-old vineyards carved into the steep hillsides

386
00:30:29,881 --> 00:30:34,060
and rugged canyons of Portugal's Douro Valley.

387
00:30:34,061 --> 00:30:38,240
Rising as high as a thousand meters above the river below, these terraces were built

388
00:30:38,241 --> 00:30:43,180
from the very rock and schist that made the Douro one of the most uncompromising landscapes

389
00:30:43,181 --> 00:30:44,640
in all of Europe.

390
00:30:44,641 --> 00:30:51,780
Yet here, in this once remote defiant place, nature and history have conspired to create

391
00:30:51,781 --> 00:30:55,040
one of the world's most treasured wines, Port.

392
00:30:55,041 --> 00:31:00,640
Well, basically Port is a fortified, what we call a fortified red wine from this region

393
00:31:00,641 --> 00:31:02,720
of the Douro Valley of Portugal.

394
00:31:02,721 --> 00:31:06,380
The grapes have been crushed just like for a normal table wine, but we've actually stopped

395
00:31:06,381 --> 00:31:10,160
the fermentation halfway through by adding what we call brandy.

396
00:31:10,161 --> 00:31:14,380
It's like a 77% alcohol by volume grappa.

397
00:31:14,381 --> 00:31:18,260
And by adding brandy, you kill the yeast off, so you end up with the wine having about half

398
00:31:18,261 --> 00:31:20,200
its natural sugar.

399
00:31:20,201 --> 00:31:24,820
So the wine is semi-sweet, with a slightly higher alcoholic content than standard red

400
00:31:24,821 --> 00:31:25,820
wine.

401
00:31:25,821 --> 00:31:32,220
No one knows who first added brandy to wine, a process now called fortification.

402
00:31:32,221 --> 00:31:37,020
But what is clear is that the impetus came not from an ancient winemaker's cellar, but

403
00:31:37,021 --> 00:31:41,520
was born of geopolitics, empires, and war.

404
00:31:41,521 --> 00:31:47,720
was actually invented by the British as much as the Portuguese.

405
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:54,120
Beginning in the 17th century, perpetual conflict with France led British merchants

406
00:31:54,121 --> 00:31:57,920
to seek out alternative sources of wine, especially in Portugal.

407
00:31:59,140 --> 00:32:02,420
Some of the original port wines were actually wine sourced on the coast of Portugal.

408
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:06,780
And as the market developed, people's tastes got more sophisticated,

409
00:32:06,781 --> 00:32:11,560
and merchants started coming upriver trying to find better wines.

410
00:32:11,740 --> 00:32:16,480
And the Douro Valley, which is some 70 or 80 miles from where we are to the coast,

411
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:19,180
the wines from this region were considered fantastic.

412
00:32:19,300 --> 00:32:22,140
The trouble is the whole trip, the journey downriver,

413
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:24,460
it then would have to be shipped a couple of days to England,

414
00:32:24,800 --> 00:32:27,420
and the wine, having come that much further, would spoil.

415
00:32:27,421 --> 00:32:28,980
So that's when they started adding brandy,

416
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:32,760
and that's what helped preserve the wine and make it famous.

417
00:32:33,760 --> 00:32:38,700
The additional brandy ensured that wine shipments from the Douro made the long journey to England.

418
00:32:41,500 --> 00:32:44,920
Continued unrest between Britain and France spurred demand for port

419
00:32:44,921 --> 00:32:50,460
and led to the rapid transformation of wine production in the Douro well into the 19th century.

420
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:55,700
Winemakers, for example, began to define proper wine-to-brandy ratios

421
00:32:55,701 --> 00:32:58,760
that not only made the port more stable,

422
00:32:58,761 --> 00:33:03,240
but also created truly unique flavors and character.

423
00:33:05,240 --> 00:33:08,200
Wines were no longer transported in resin-lined goatskins,

424
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:11,260
but rather in large barrels known as pipes,

425
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:13,840
still the standard measure of port today.

426
00:33:15,420 --> 00:33:18,800
In the spring, these pipes were loaded aboard small skiffs,

427
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:23,120
known as robloves, for the dangerous journey from Doro to Oporto,

428
00:33:23,580 --> 00:33:25,500
from where they were shipped to England and abroad.

429
00:33:28,760 --> 00:33:35,760
Today, a new revolution is underway, led by companies like the Symington family estates,

430
00:33:35,761 --> 00:33:39,760
the world's leading port producer.

431
00:33:39,761 --> 00:33:44,760
The Symington family can trace their roots in the industry back 13 generations, to the

432
00:33:44,761 --> 00:33:48,760
very founding of the port trade in the 1600s.

433
00:33:48,761 --> 00:33:56,760
They now oversee 24 quintas, where famous ports like Dows, Bohr and Grams are born.

434
00:33:56,761 --> 00:34:02,780
We've got about three million vines now under our management and ownership.

435
00:34:04,600 --> 00:34:09,700
And while they're fully aware of port's rich heritage, their focus is very much on the future.

436
00:34:10,560 --> 00:34:12,960
This is the Quintedurge Malvierdurge Winery.

437
00:34:13,320 --> 00:34:18,160
Malvierdurge is a property that has been associated with the Graham port brand since 1890.

438
00:34:19,080 --> 00:34:23,900
And these buildings were where the original port was made from the Malvierdurge estate.

439
00:34:23,901 --> 00:34:28,520
traditionally port was made by foot treading and a bunch of people about 20

440
00:34:28,521 --> 00:34:33,900
people would jump into a tank and literally stomp it by foot.

441
00:34:33,901 --> 00:34:38,780
Foot treading was first practiced by the ancient Romans who recognized that small

442
00:34:38,781 --> 00:34:43,040
thick-skinned grapes like those grown in the Douro needed to be crushed firmly

443
00:34:43,041 --> 00:34:49,140
and quickly to initiate fermentation and extract the optimum flavor and color.

444
00:34:49,141 --> 00:34:53,780
traditional presses simply did not apply the same direct pressure as human feet

445
00:34:53,781 --> 00:34:59,140
which ground the grapes into the floor of the stone tank known as a lagarde in

446
00:34:59,141 --> 00:35:04,660
the 1960s the Simington's installed an auto vinification system that uses

447
00:35:04,661 --> 00:35:09,580
pressure built up during fermentation to maximize extraction but for the finest

448
00:35:09,581 --> 00:35:16,100
ports the system just couldn't match the juice tread by foot and so the family

449
00:35:16,101 --> 00:35:20,760
made a long-term commitment to design and build a robotic lagar.

450
00:35:20,761 --> 00:35:25,020
It has four plates that slide over each other hydraulically.

451
00:35:25,021 --> 00:35:28,100
There are silicon toes on the end of the plates.

452
00:35:28,101 --> 00:35:30,020
And those silicon toes will literally

453
00:35:30,021 --> 00:35:33,020
grind the skins against the floor of the tank.

454
00:35:33,021 --> 00:35:34,940
The feet themselves are temperature controlled.

455
00:35:34,941 --> 00:35:36,600
They're heated up to 37 degrees centigrade,

456
00:35:36,601 --> 00:35:38,040
the same as the human leg.

457
00:35:38,041 --> 00:35:40,420
So you've got a warming effect in the feet.

458
00:35:40,421 --> 00:35:42,140
They also can work all night if they have to,

459
00:35:42,141 --> 00:35:45,640
whereas the humans would only do it for two or three hours in the late evening.

460
00:35:46,900 --> 00:35:50,240
Once fermentation has brought the wine to the desired sugar levels,

461
00:35:50,940 --> 00:35:53,700
the fermenting wine is drained into a storage vat,

462
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:55,840
where the neutral-flavored brandy is added.

463
00:35:57,260 --> 00:36:02,020
We add about one part of brandy at 77% alcohol by volume

464
00:36:02,021 --> 00:36:07,320
to about four parts of must, which is at about 7% alcohol by volume.

465
00:36:07,321 --> 00:36:14,000
So if you put that if you do the math it works out a wine about 19 and a half 20 percent alcohol for the finished blend

466
00:36:15,940 --> 00:36:22,700
Once it is settled the wine will travel down river where it will be judged for flavor clarity and other characteristics

467
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:29,000
Well, we'll take some of these lots and then we'll put them on the bench

468
00:36:29,001 --> 00:36:31,840
And we'll try and put them together in such a way to make something really great

469
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:35,220
Occasionally, you'll have one individual blend, which is great,

470
00:36:35,580 --> 00:36:39,580
but we believe, like, you know, two plus two can add up to five in our business.

471
00:36:39,700 --> 00:36:43,540
You know, you can really put two things together and make something even better than some of the parts.

472
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:49,020
In general, port can be divided into two fundamental styles.

473
00:36:50,040 --> 00:36:53,380
Ruby port is aged in oak, then bottled after three years,

474
00:36:53,381 --> 00:36:56,580
and features a bright red color and general fruitfulness.

475
00:36:56,581 --> 00:37:05,340
Conversely, tawny ports are aged in oak at least six years, during which time their color

476
00:37:05,341 --> 00:37:08,140
and nature transform.

477
00:37:08,141 --> 00:37:12,900
The longer you leave it in barrel, the wine goes from that purpley-red color and it fades

478
00:37:12,901 --> 00:37:18,620
into a lovely nutty kind of amber-brown color.

479
00:37:18,621 --> 00:37:23,540
In rare years, when all the conditions are exactly right, port winemakers will declare

480
00:37:23,541 --> 00:37:26,540
a vintage year.

481
00:37:26,541 --> 00:37:31,200
These wines spend only two years in oak before bottling, where they will be left to mature

482
00:37:31,201 --> 00:37:33,540
for decades.

483
00:37:33,541 --> 00:37:40,300
Vintages such as the 1970 and 1955 Grams are among the most cherished wines in the world,

484
00:37:40,301 --> 00:37:45,600
not simply for their flavor, but also for the sense of history and place they evoke.

485
00:37:45,601 --> 00:37:52,780
And for some, those are qualities to be treasured above all else, especially in one of the world's

486
00:37:52,781 --> 00:37:55,420
great cellars.

487
00:37:55,421 --> 00:38:01,360
Portugal is not just famous for wine, but also for its cork oak trees that supply half

488
00:38:01,361 --> 00:38:07,080
of all the world's corks, nearly 7 billion a year.

489
00:38:07,081 --> 00:38:14,620
